---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { DEMO_URL } from '../config/site';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do I need to buy a POS machine?',
    a: 'No. It runs in a browser, so the phone in your pocket or the laptop on the counter is the terminal. If you already have a receipt printer or a barcode scanner they will usually work, and if you do not, you can start without either.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this the same as the POS machine my bank gave me?',
    a: 'No, and the two do different jobs. A bank POS terminal takes a card payment and settles it to your account. This records the sale: what left the shelf, at what price, by which staff member, and what that does to your stock and your takings. Most shops that use both keep both.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it handle Mobile Money?',
    a: 'It records it, the same way the answer above describes. The customer still pays on your own MoMo number, and the sale is marked as Mobile Money, cash or card so the takings report separates them and you can reconcile at the end of the day.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if the internet goes off in the middle of a sale?',
    a: 'The cashier can keep selling. The sale is held on that device and goes up when you press Sync. There are limits worth knowing about, and they are written out in full on the security and uptime page.',
  },
  {
    /* Was "Single Branch, up to three tills; Multi-Branch, up to five
       locations; above that we quote it". Those tiers went on 8 October
       2026, and nothing in the product ever counted tills. */
    q: 'How many people can sell at once?',
    a: 'As many as you have tills for. There is no limit on tills in a branch. One branch is included in the yearly price, and each additional branch is GHS 200 a year.',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout page="pos-system-ghana" faqs={faqs} faqHeading="Questions people ask about the POS">
    <p>
        Most POS software sold in Ghana was designed somewhere else and priced somewhere else.
        You get a monthly fee in dollars, a card reader that assumes every customer taps, and a
        support line that opens when your shop is closing. Then the network drops and the whole
        thing stops.
    </p>

    <h2>It runs on what you already own</h2>
    <p>
        There is no terminal to buy and nothing to install. Open the browser on a phone, a tablet
        or the laptop on the counter and you are at the till. That matters more than it sounds:
        the usual reason a shop puts off changing systems is the hardware bill at the front, and
        here there is not one.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you already have a thermal receipt printer or a barcode scanner, they generally work.
        If you do not, you can run the whole thing off a phone and print nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>Mobile Money is a payment method, not an afterthought</h2>
    <p>
        A sale can be recorded as Mobile Money, cash or card, and the day's takings separate them
        so the reconciliation at closing is a comparison rather than a puzzle. Prices are in cedis
        because the shop is in Ghana, not converted from a dollar list at whatever rate applied
        the day the page was built.
    </p>

    <h2>The till tells you what the shelf is doing</h2>
    <p>
        Every sale moves stock. That is the entire point of putting the two in one system: you
        stop reconciling a sales app against a stock book, because there is nothing to reconcile.
        Sell the last four of something and the reorder alert fires the same moment, not at the
        end of the week when someone gets round to counting.
    </p>
    <p>
        More on that on the <a href="/inventory-management-software/">inventory side of it</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>It keeps selling when the network does not</h2>
    <p>
        Connectivity in Ghana is good until it is not, usually at the worst time. When the
        connection drops mid-trade the cashier can keep taking payment. The sale is held on that
        device and syncs when you press Sync.
    </p>
    <div class="erp-page-note">
        <strong>The limits, so they are not a surprise.</strong> Sync is manual, not automatic.
        The queue lives on the device that took the sale, so a different phone cannot recover it.
        Only selling is queued, so stock adjustments and purchase orders still need a connection.
        The whole of it is written out on <a href="/security-and-uptime/">security and uptime</a>.
    </div>

    <h2>You can see who sold what</h2>
    <p>
        Every sale carries the staff member who rang it up. So can every discount, every void and
        every refund. Shop owners rarely ask for this in those words. What they say is that the
        takings do not match what went out of the door, and this is the part of the system that
        answers it.
    </p>

    {/* One plan, paid before use, since 8 October 2026. The figures are the
        homepage's and the app's; change them there first. */}
    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
        GHS 1,000 a year with one branch and every feature, plus GHS 200 a year for each
        additional branch. There is a one-time setup fee of GHS 1,000, paid with your first year,
        so one branch is GHS 2,000 to start. There is no free trial: the
        <a href={DEMO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-track="demo_open" data-track-where="page-body">live demo</a>
        is the way to try it first, and it needs no account. The detail is on the <a href="/#erp-pricing">pricing section of the homepage</a>.
    </p>
</PageLayout>
